  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Buchwaldoboletus lignicola, commonly known as the Wood Bolete, is a small to medium-sized fungus in the Boletaceae family characterized by its distinctive wood-decomposing lifestyle. This species is notable for its ecological role as a saprotroph, breaking down decaying wood in forest ecosystems throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Wood substrate requirement",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies emerge directly from decaying logs, stumps, or buried wood rather than from soil, which is highly distinctive and diagnostic for this species",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore surface color",
      "description": "The underside features small, angular pores that are typically yellow to olive-yellow, bruising or staining greenish-brown when handled",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap appearance",
      "description": "Small brown to tan cap (1-4 cm) with a dry to slightly viscid surface, often irregular in shape due to the uneven wood substrate",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Wood Boletes are found exclusively on decaying hardwood logs, stumps, and woody debris in mixed and deciduous forests throughout New England, particularly in moist, shaded conditions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike most boletes that form mycorrhizal relationships with tree roots, Buchwaldoboletus lignicola is a saprotroph that specializes in breaking down dead wood, making it an important recycler of forest nutrients.",
